<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>dev.mainframe</groupId>
    <artifactId>mainframe-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>mainframe-template</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>MainFrame Templates</name>
  <description>Starting a project from a template: new vexel-desktop</description>

  <!--
    A capability, so a module, and the boundary is drawn inside it as well as
    around it. Two packages:

      dev.mainframe.template        the engine. Reads a template, works out what
                                    files it would write, and refuses the ones it
                                    should not. Imports nothing from MainFrame,
                                    and nothing from anywhere else either: a JDK
                                    and a folder of templates is the whole of it.
      dev.mainframe.template.shell  the adapter. Turns slots into MainFrame form
                                    fields, answers into a record, and the result
                                    into a Plan, so `new` gets the same dry-run,
                                    the same confirmation and the same errors as
                                    every other command that writes.

    The could-another-project-use-it test is asked of the first package, and it
    passes: Scaffold and Checks would drop into an installer or a Maven mojo
    unchanged. The second package is the join, and is not meant to be reusable,
    which is why it is a package of its own rather than mixed through the engine.

    The price of that line is paid in small duplication: the engine parses its own
    manifest rather than borrowing the shell's reader, and matches its own globs.
    That price is the proof the boundary is real.
  -->
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>dev.mainframe</groupId>
      <artifactId>mainframe-core</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
</project>
